  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 151 AM EDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nSunday and beyond, north and central Georgia will remain within a\nregime of northwesterly flow aloft, with weakening high pressure \nat the surface. Multiple pushes of supremely dry, Canadian \ncontinental air will rotate around what remains of a potent \nNor'easter and into the Southeast, with PWATs progged to fall \nbetween the 10th and 25th percentiles climatologically (generally \nless than three-quarters of an inch, with more seasonable values \nexpected on Sunday). The combination of uncharacteristically dry \nair and subsidence from lingering surface high pressure will keep \nPoPs near-negligible through midweek next week (<15%, which is the\ndelineator between dry and a mention of isolated rain chances). \nModeration of temperatures will occur as the airmass stagnates, \nwith afternoon highs rising back into the 80s areawide -- back to \n5 to 10 degrees above average by next Tuesday. Despite this, \nmorning lows will be crisp and positively fall-like (finally), in \nthe 50s to lower 60s for most.",
